---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="ExCSV — CSV, but self-describing" wide>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-text">
      <h1>CSV, but self-describing</h1>
      <p class="tagline">
        Built for data analysts and everyone wrangling large tabular datasets —
        schema, stats, and provenance travel with the file, not in a separate README.
        ExCSV adds inline metadata, SQL companions, and aggregations to plain CSV.
        Zero dependencies. CLI-friendly. Backward-compatible.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="/spec/" class="btn btn-primary">Read the spec</a>
        <a href="/variants/" class="btn btn-secondary">Storage forms</a>
        <a href="/examples/" class="btn btn-secondary">See examples</a>
        <a href="/tools/" class="btn btn-secondary">Tools</a>
      </div>
      <p class="version-tag">v0.3 — Draft</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-code">
      <pre><code><span class="hl-meta">#!excsv version=0.3 delim=comma header=1 sql-dialect=postgres</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#@source: analytics_db.events</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=date type=date</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=event type=string</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=count type=int</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#$ddl: CREATE TABLE events (date DATE, event TEXT, count INTEGER)</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#%sum: ,,1250</span>
date,event,count
2026-01-15,signup,320
2026-01-15,purchase,180
2026-01-16,signup,410
2026-01-16,purchase,340</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Features -->
  <section class="features">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Backward-compatible</h3>
      <p>Any CSV reader can consume the data section. Metadata lines are comments. Nothing breaks.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Sidecar for legacy CSV</h3>
      <p>Keep <code>data.csv</code> unchanged. Put schema, SQL, and stats in <code>data.excsv</code> with <code>reference=data.csv</code> — metadata only, no duplicated rows.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>CLI-friendly</h3>
      <p>All metadata lines start with <code>#</code>, so <code>grep</code>, <code>awk</code>, <code>head</code>, and <code>cut</code> work on the data section out of the box.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Built-in aggregations</h3>
      <p>Embed sum, avg, count, min/max, and more as metadata rows. Consumers get stats without scanning the data.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/variants/zipped/">Zipped</a> distribution</h3>
      <p><code>.excsv.zip</code> and <code>.extsv.zip</code> archives carry their summary in the ZIP comment — read schema and aggregations without unzipping. No extraction needed for preview.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3><a href="/variants/pack/">Multi-table pack</a></h3>
      <p><code>.excsv.pack.zip</code> — multi-table, columnar: manifest + <code>.col</code> files per column. Selective reads and database snapshots without row-oriented ZIP overhead.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Schema annotations</h3>
      <p>Define column types, constraints, defaults, and validation patterns inline — or in a <a href="/variants/sidecar/">sidecar</a> next to plain CSV.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>SQL companions</h3>
      <p>Ship DDL and DQL alongside data, tagged per dialect (<code>mysql</code>, <code>postgres</code>, <code>clickhouse</code>...). Recreate the schema in any database.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- LLM-friendly -->
  <section class="llm">
    <div class="llm-head">
      <span class="llm-badge">LLM-friendly</span>
      <h2>Made for AI assistants, too</h2>
      <p class="llm-lead">
        Paste an ExCSV table into a chat and the model gets the schema, types, units, and
        precomputed stats up front — no guessing delimiters, inferring types from a few rows,
        or recomputing totals. The metadata an LLM needs to reason correctly travels with the data.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="llm-grid">
      <div class="llm-point">
        <h4>Explicit types &amp; units</h4>
        <p>Columns declare <code>type</code>, <code>unit</code>, and <code>format</code>, so an LLM reads <code>1050.50</code> as USD — not a count — and never misparses ZIP codes or dates.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="llm-point">
        <h4>Aggregations as a trust anchor</h4>
        <p><code>#%</code> rows carry sum, avg, count, and distinct counts. A model answers aggregate questions — and sanity-checks its own math — without scanning every row.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="llm-point">
        <h4>Row grain, spelled out</h4>
        <p><code>#@grain: one row per order</code> tells the model what a single row means, instead of forcing it to reverse-engineer that from column names.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="llm-point">
        <h4>Enums &amp; analytical roles</h4>
        <p><code>enum=</code> gives the closed value domain; <code>role=</code> (id / dimension / measure / time) and <code>agg=</code> let a model pick correct operations — group by dimensions, sum measures, never sum an id.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <pre class="llm-code"><code><span class="hl-meta">#@grain: one row per order</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=order_id type=int role=id</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=status type=string role=dimension enum=pending|completed|cancelled</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#column name=amount type=decimal unit=USD role=measure agg=sum</span>
<span class="hl-meta">#%sum: ,,1050.50</span></code></pre>
  </section>

  <!-- Why -->
  <section class="why">
    <h2>Why ExCSV?</h2>
    <p class="why-lead">
      Tabular exports are everywhere, but the context around them rarely is. Analysts and data engineers
      lose hours inferring column types, re-scanning huge files for row counts and sums, and rebuilding
      database schemas from scratch. ExCSV targets that friction: self-describing files that still open
      in any CSV tool.
    </p>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div>
        <h4>Progressive enhancement</h4>
        <p>Start with plain CSV. Add a header line. Add column types when you need them. Add aggregations or SQL when you want them. Every layer is optional.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>No JSON required</h4>
        <p>Metadata is line-oriented key-value, not a nested structure. Easy to read, easy to write, easy to diff.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Multi-dialect SQL</h4>
        <p>One file can ship MySQL, PostgreSQL, and ClickHouse DDL side-by-side. Pick the right flavor when you load the data into a database.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Self-describing files</h4>
        <p>The file carries its own schema, provenance, and statistics. No external metadata files, no guessing column types.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Prior art -->
  <section class="inspired-by">
    <h2>Inspired by</h2>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div>
        <h4><a href="https://docs.astropy.org/en/stable/io/ascii/ecsv.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ECSV</a> — Astropy's Enhanced Character-Separated Values</h4>
        <p>Showed that a CSV file can carry a versioned signature, per-column schema, and table-level metadata as <code>#</code>-prefixed lines and still round-trip through every plain CSV reader. ExCSV adopts the same shape but trades nested YAML for line-oriented <code>key=value</code> pairs — friendlier to <code>grep</code>, <code>diff</code>, and humans.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4><a href="https://docs.influxdata.com/influxdb/v2/reference/syntax/annotated-csv/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Annotated CSV</a> — InfluxDB</h4>
        <p>Established the "one annotation row, one value per data column" pattern with <code>#datatype</code>, <code>#group</code>, and <code>#default</code> rows. ExCSV's <code>#%</code> aggregation rows (<code>sum</code>, <code>avg</code>, <code>min</code>, ...) inherit this layout directly.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4><a href="https://github.com/MetaCSV/MetaCSV" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MetaCSV</a> — sidecar <code>.mcsv</code></h4>
        <p>Typed metadata in a sibling CSV (<code>domain,key,value</code>) beside plain <code>data.csv</code>. ExCSV's <a href="/variants/sidecar/">sidecar</a> pursues the same goal with <code>#</code> lines and <code>reference=</code> instead of a second CSV vocabulary.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="full-credit">Full credit and a translation guide on the <a href="/spec/#prior-art">specification</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 3rem 0 4rem;
  }
  .hero h1 {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    margin-top: 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }
  .tagline {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem 0.65rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .version-tag {
    margin-top: 1.2rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    padding: 0.55em 1.1em;
    border-radius: 8px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: opacity 0.15s, border-color 0.15s;
  }
  .btn:hover { text-decoration: none; opacity: 0.9; }
  .btn-primary {
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: #fff;
  }
  .btn-primary:hover {
    opacity: 1;
    filter: brightness(1.05);
  }
  .btn-secondary {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    color: var(--color-text);
    background: var(--color-bg);
  }
  .btn-secondary:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-muted);
    opacity: 1;
  }
  .hero-code pre {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .features {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 3rem;
  }
  .card {
    background: var(--color-card-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }
  .card h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }
  .card p {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .llm {
    padding: 2rem 2rem 2.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 12px;
    background: var(--color-card-bg);
  }
  .llm-badge {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.2em 0.7em;
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }
  .llm-head h2 {
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
  }
  .llm-lead {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 52rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.75rem;
  }
  .llm-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1.25rem 1.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }
  .llm-point h4 {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
  }
  .llm-point p {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .llm-code {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .why {
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .why h2 {
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .why-lead {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 52rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.75rem;
  }
  .grid-2 {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  .grid-2 h4 { margin-top: 0; }
  .grid-2 h4 a {
    color: var(--color-text);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .grid-2 h4 a:hover { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .grid-2 p {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
  .inspired-by {
    padding: 1rem 0 2.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .inspired-by h2 {
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .full-credit {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 960px) {
    .hero {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2rem;
      padding: 2rem 0 3rem;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .hero h1 { font-size: 1.8rem; }
    .features { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .grid-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .llm-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .llm { padding: 1.5rem; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .cta-row {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
    }
    .btn {
      width: 100%;
      justify-content: center;
    }
  }
</style>
